```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router_core_1
diagnosis_text:
The OSPF adjacency failure between router_core_1 and router_core_3 is caused by
router_core_1's eth1 interface being in a link-down state. Ethtool on
router_core_1:eth1 explicitly reports "Link detected: no", and the kernel
interface flags show <BROADCAST,MULTICAST> with no UP or LOWER_UP bit — meaning
the interface is not passing any L2 traffic. Because FRR's OSPF process only
activates on interfaces that are operationally UP, eth1 is entirely absent from
router_core_1's OSPF interface table, explaining why router_core_1 lists no
neighbor toward router_core_3. On the opposite end, router_core_3:eth0 is fully
UP (LOWER_UP), has 172.16.0.3/31 configured correctly, and at the time of
diagnosis still showed a stale Full adjacency toward 172.16.0.2 with a
dwindling Dead timer (~15 s left) — consistent with a link that dropped very
recently. The OSPF configuration on router_core_1 is correct: the network
statement "network 172.16.0.2/31 area 0" is present in the running config and
the IP address 172.16.0.2/31 is bound to eth1. Only the physical/virtual layer
is broken. Root cause: router_core_1:eth1 was brought down (administratively or
via virtual-link disconnection), severing L1 on that side while router_core_3's
end remained up. Corrective fix: run "ip link set eth1 up" on router_core_1; the
OSPF adjacency will re-establish automatically within one Hello interval (10 s)
and backbone redundancy will be restored.
=== END ===
```